<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaub</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaub is a small wine town on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, in the Upper Middle Rhine Valley. The island toll castle of Pfalzgrafenstein in the river beside it collected Rhine tolls for the Electoral Palatinate, and on New Year's night 1813–1814 the Prussian field marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> crossed the Rhine there in pursuit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. After 1918 it lay in the Free State Bottleneck.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaub.jpg" alt="Kaub" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: imehling · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaub,_Castle_Gutenfels,_Castle_Pfalzgrafenstein_-_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
